---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../styles/interactive.css';
import MailerLiteSignup from '../../../components/MailerLiteSignup.astro';
import { SUPPORTER_COPY } from '../../../lib/app-config';
---
<Base title="Cold Hold | ReadyGearHub" description="Log fridge and freezer temperatures during a power outage, with official FDA guidance. Works offline on this device.">
  <link slot="head" rel="manifest" href="/apps/cold-hold/manifest.webmanifest" />
  <link slot="head" rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apps/cold-hold/icon-192.png" />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/apps/">Apps</a> / Cold Hold</p>
      <h1>Cold Hold</h1>
      <p class="lead">Log fridge and freezer temperatures during a power outage. Official FDA figures stay on the same screen.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <aside class="alert-disclaimer" role="note">
      <p><strong>This is a manual log, not a connected thermometer or alarm.</strong> It does not measure temperature, detect a power outage on its own, or decide whether food is safe to eat. Keep appliance thermometers in the fridge and freezer, and follow official guidance from the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/food-and-water-safety-during-power-outages-and-floods">FDA</a> and <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/food-safety-during-power-outage">FoodSafety.gov</a>.</p>
      <p>This is not medical, legal, or emergency advice. If someone may have eaten unsafe food, contact a medical professional or poison control.</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="tool-actions no-print" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <button type="button" class="btn" id="install-app" hidden>Install Cold Hold</button>
    </div>
    <aside class="card no-print" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <h2>Need a quick FoodSafety.gov countdown?</h2>
      <p>For a browser countdown based on FoodSafety.gov’s 4-hour fridge and 24- or 48-hour freezer windows, use the <a href="/tools/outage-timer/">fridge and freezer outage timer</a>. Cold Hold is here when you want to log the actual temperatures you read.</p>
      <p class="card-actions"><a class="btn small ghost" href="/tools/outage-timer/">Open the outage timer</a></p>
    </aside>
    <div class="tool-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Cold Hold sections">
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-status" id="tab-status">Status</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-log" id="tab-log">Log</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-guide" id="tab-guide">Guidance</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-setup" id="tab-setup">Setup</button>
    </div>

    <section id="panel-status" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-status">
      <div id="outage-bar" class="status-banner" hidden>
        <p class="fine">Power-out log in progress</p>
        <p class="app-stat" id="outage-clock">—</p>
        <p class="fine" id="outage-started"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn" id="start-outage">Power is out</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="end-outage" hidden>Power is back</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="log-temp">Log a temperature</button>
      </div>
      <div class="timer-grid" id="appliance-grid" style="margin-top:16px"></div>
    </section>

    <section id="panel-log" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-log" hidden>
      <h2>Temperature history</h2>
      <ul class="log-list" id="reading-list"></ul>
      <h2>Outage history</h2>
      <ul class="log-list" id="outage-list"></ul>
    </section>

    <section id="panel-guide" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-guide" hidden class="prose">
      <h2>Official figures this app uses</h2>
      <p>Quoted and summarized from U.S. FDA pages. Always read the original. These are general consumer tips, not a guarantee for your appliances or food.</p>
      <blockquote>“The refrigerator will keep food cold for about 4 hours if it is unopened.”</blockquote>
      <blockquote>“A full freezer will keep the temperature for approximately 48 hours (24 hours if it is half full) if the door remains closed.”</blockquote>
      <blockquote>“If the freezer thermometer reads 40°F or below, the food is safe and may be refrozen.” If there was no thermometer, “If the food still contains ice crystals or is 40°F or below, it is safe to refreeze or cook.”</blockquote>
      <blockquote>“Discard any refrigerated perishable food (such as meat, poultry, seafood, milk, eggs, or leftovers) that has been at refrigerator temperatures above 40°F for 4 hours or more.”</blockquote>
      <p class="fine">
        Source: <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/food-and-water-safety-during-power-outages-and-floods">FDA: Food and Water Safety During Power Outages and Floods</a>.
        Related: <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-during-emergencies/power-outages-key-tips-consumers-about-food-safety">FDA key tips</a>
        and <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/food-safety-during-power-outage">FoodSafety.gov power-outage charts</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>Usual storage targets (when power is on)</h2>
      <p>FDA: check that the freezer is at or below 0°F and the refrigerator is at or below 40°F, and use appliance thermometers so you can still judge safety if the power later goes out. (Same FDA outage page, “Before a Power Outage / Emergency…”)</p>
      <h2>What this screen will not do</h2>
      <p>It will not tell you to throw food away or to keep it. Appearance and odor are not reliable tests (FDA). A rising reading in this log only means you should look at official guidance and at the food and thermometers in front of you.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="panel-setup" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-setup" hidden>
      <form class="tool-toolbar cols-2" id="units-form">
        <label>Units
          <select name="units">
            <option value="F">Fahrenheit</option>
            <option value="C">Celsius</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <div class="tool-actions"><button type="submit" class="btn">Save units</button></div>
      </form>
      <p class="fine">Official FDA figures are in °F. Celsius values are conversions for display only (40°F = 4.4°C, 0°F = −17.8°C).</p>
      <h2>Appliances</h2>
      <ul class="log-list" id="appliance-list"></ul>
      <div class="tool-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="add-appliance">Add appliance</button>
      </div>
      <article class="card" style="margin-top:16px">
        <h2>Backup</h2>
        <div class="tool-actions">
          <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="export-json">Download JSON</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="import-json">Import JSON</button>
        </div>
        <input id="import-file" type="file" accept="application/json,.json" hidden />
      </article>
      <article class="card" id="pro-note">
        <h2>Support ReadyGearHub</h2>
        <p>{SUPPORTER_COPY.intro} On Cold Hold, that could include more than two appliances and CSV or print exports. {SUPPORTER_COPY.noPayments}</p>
      </article>
    </section>
    <div class="prose no-print" style="margin-top:32px">
      <h2>Fridge and freezer questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details><summary>How long is food in the fridge safe without power?</summary><p>About 4 hours if the door stays closed, per FoodSafety.gov and the FDA. After that, discard perishable refrigerated food such as meat, poultry, seafood, milk, eggs, and leftovers that has been above 40°F for 4 hours or more.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How long will a freezer keep food frozen?</summary><p>About 48 hours for a full freezer and about 24 hours for a half-full one, as long as the door stays closed.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I refreeze food that started to thaw?</summary><p>Yes, if it still has ice crystals or reads 40°F or below on a thermometer, per the FDA. Otherwise, cook it right away or discard it.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I tell by smell or look whether food is safe?</summary><p>No. Appearance and odor are not reliable tests. Never taste food to check it. When in doubt, throw it out.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What helps before the next outage?</summary><p>Keep appliance thermometers in the fridge (40°F or below) and freezer (0°F or below), keep a few frozen water jugs or ice packs in the freezer, and keep the doors closed once the power goes out.</p></details>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">Sources: <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/food-safety-during-power-outage">FoodSafety.gov</a> and <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/food-and-water-safety-during-power-outages-and-floods">FDA</a>. General guidance, not a guarantee for your own appliances or food.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="no-print">
      <MailerLiteSignup
        variant="card"
        heading="Want a printable fridge & freezer safety card plus quiet storm-readiness reminders?"
        intro="Leave your email and we'll hand you our free printable storm checklist, with the fridge and freezer times on one page you can tape inside a cabinet. A few times a season we'll send a calm reminder to check your thermometers and kit."
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<script>
  import { initColdHold } from '../../../scripts/cold-hold';
  initColdHold();
</script>
